IMPORT prelude

-- Foreign National Emblems (Control of Display) Act 1949 (Singapore) -- what counts as a foreign national emblem and as display "in
-- public" (ss 2, 3(2)), the ban on displaying one in public or at a school (s 3(1)), the exceptions and Ministerial exemptions (ss 4,
-- 5), police powers of arrest and entry (ss 6, 7), and the offences, penalties and Public Prosecutor's consent (s 8).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/FNECDA1949.txt. The deposit says it "incorporates all
-- amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021", and it carries annotations of
-- Act 29 of 2022 with effect from 1 August 2023 (the latest amendment annotated). The deposit's metadata calls it the current version
-- as at 3 October 2026. NOTE: the Arrangement of Sections at the top of the deposit is out of step with the body (it numbers "Short
-- title" as 2 and leaves "Offences and penalties" unnumbered). The BODY's numbering is followed here: s 1 short title, s 2
-- interpretation, s 3 prohibition, s 4 exceptions, s 5 exemptions, s 6 arrest, s 7 entry and search, s 8 offences and penalties.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0131 in subjects/sg/requirements.jsonl: tier 2 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by
-- everyday-life relevance. The requirement asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked
-- a sharper question yet. The Act is short (eight sections), so it is encoded nearly whole.
--
-- Not encoded: s 1 (short title); the content of any Ministerial order or permit under s 5 (only whether one covers the display and
-- whether its conditions were kept); what counts as "displaying" (the Act does not define it -- whether wearing a shirt bearing a
-- likeness is a display is left to the fact-finder); whether the exequatur clause in s 4(a)(i) qualifies diplomatic as well as
-- consular representatives; and a breach of a permit condition by a display that s 3 would not otherwise catch.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Foreign National Emblems (Control of Display) Act 1949 -- display`

§§ `2 -- foreign national emblem, school`

-- s 2: "foreign national emblem" means "any flag, banner or other emblem being or purporting to be the flag, banner or other emblem
-- of any state, or the flag, banner or other emblem of any political organisation claiming to be a national movement in any state
-- or any likeness or resemblance however reproduced of any national leader or former national leader of any state or the leader or
-- former leader of any such political organisation".
-- "state" means "any state or country, other than Singapore, and includes any dependency, possession or part of any state".
-- "school" means "any premises or any part of any premises where persons are habitually taught whether in one or more classes".
DECLARE Emblem IS ONE OF
    `the flag of a foreign country`
    `a flag purporting to be that of a foreign country`
    `the flag of a dependency or part of a foreign country`
    `the banner of a political organisation claiming to be a national movement in a foreign country`
    `a likeness of a current or former foreign national leader`
    `a likeness of a current or former leader of such an organisation`
    `the Singapore flag`
    `a football club scarf`
    `a likeness of a foreign film star`

GIVEN `the emblem` IS A Emblem
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a foreign national emblem` IF
    CONSIDER `the emblem`
    WHEN `the Singapore flag` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a football club scarf` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a likeness of a foreign film star` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `persons are habitually taught there` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the premises are a school` IF
    `persons are habitually taught there`

§§ `3 -- the prohibition`

-- s 3(1): "No person shall display in public or at or within any school any foreign national emblem."
-- s 3(2): displayed in public "if it is displayed in any road, street, bridge, passage, footway or place over which the public or any
-- class of the public has a right of way or to which the public has access, whether on payment or otherwise, or if it is displayed in
-- such manner as to be visible from any such road, street, bridge, passage, footway or place by any member of the public".
DECLARE Displayer IS ONE OF
    `a member of the public`
    `an accredited diplomat or consul granted an exequatur or authorisation`
    `an officially recognised representative of a Commonwealth or Irish government`
    `a person with immunities under Part 2 or 3 of the Second Schedule to the International Organisations Act`

DECLARE Display HAS
    `the emblem` IS A Emblem
    `the displayer` IS A Displayer
    `it is the emblem of the displayer's own state` IS A BOOLEAN
    `in a place the public has a right of way over or access to` IS A BOOLEAN
    `visible from such a place by a member of the public` IS A BOOLEAN
    `at or within a school` IS A BOOLEAN
    `on a ship or aircraft` IS A BOOLEAN
    `covered by a Minister's order or permit` IS A BOOLEAN
    `within the order's or permit's conditions` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the display` IS A Display
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is displayed in public` IF
        `the display`'s `in a place the public has a right of way over or access to`
    OR  `the display`'s `visible from such a place by a member of the public`

GIVEN `the display` IS A Display
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 3 catches the display` IF
        `is a foreign national emblem` (`the display`'s `the emblem`)
    AND (    `is displayed in public` `the display`
         OR  `the display`'s `at or within a school`)

§§ `4 and 5 -- exceptions and exemptions`

-- s 4: "Nothing in section 3 shall apply to -- (a) the display of any foreign national emblem of the state of which he is a national"
-- by (i) an accredited diplomatic or consular representative "to whom an exequatur or provisional or other authorisation has been
-- granted by the Government"; (ii) a person in the service of a Commonwealth Government, a British dependency or possession, or the
-- Republic of Ireland "officially recognised as representing that government in Singapore"; (iii) a person on whom immunities and
-- privileges under Part 2 or Part 3 of the Second Schedule to the International Organisations (Immunities and Privileges) Act 1948
-- have been conferred; or "(b) the display of any foreign national emblem on any ship or aircraft".
-- s 5: the Minister may, "by order published in the Gazette or by a permit in writing", exempt a display from s 3, and may regulate
-- "the manner of that display and the period during which that display may be permitted".
GIVEN `the displayer` IS A Displayer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a section 4(a) person` IF
    CONSIDER `the displayer`
    WHEN `a member of the public` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the display` IS A Display
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 4 excepts the display` IF
        (    `is a section 4(a) person` (`the display`'s `the displayer`)
         AND `the display`'s `it is the emblem of the displayer's own state`)
    OR  `the display`'s `on a ship or aircraft`

GIVEN `the display` IS A Display
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 5 exempts the display` IF
        `the display`'s `covered by a Minister's order or permit`
    AND `the display`'s `within the order's or permit's conditions`

§§ `8 -- offences`

-- s 8(1): an offence for any person who "(a) displays any foreign national emblem contrary to the provisions of this Act or of any
-- condition of any order or permit made or given under section 5; or (b) being the owner, tenant, lessee, occupier or person in charge
-- of any premises knowingly permits the display of any foreign national emblem on or from those premises or within any school contrary
-- to the provisions of this Act" or of any such condition.
GIVEN `the display` IS A Display
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the display is an offence` IF
        `section 3 catches the display` `the display`
    AND (NOT `section 4 excepts the display` `the display`)
    AND (NOT `section 5 exempts the display` `the display`)

GIVEN `the display` IS A Display, `is the owner, tenant, lessee, occupier or person in charge` IS A BOOLEAN, `knowingly permits it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the occupier commits an offence` IF
        `the display is an offence` `the display`
    AND `is the owner, tenant, lessee, occupier or person in charge`
    AND `knowingly permits it`

§§ `8 -- penalties and prosecution`

-- s 8(1): liable on conviction "(i) in the case of a person other than a body corporate to a fine not exceeding $500 or to imprisonment
-- for a term not exceeding 6 months or to both; and (ii) in the case of a body corporate to a fine not exceeding $1,000."
-- s 8(2): "No prosecution shall be instituted under this Act without the prior consent in writing of the Public Prosecutor."
DECLARE Offender IS ONE OF
    `a person other than a body corporate`
    `a body corporate`

GIVEN `the offender` IS A Offender
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine in dollars` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offender`
    WHEN `a body corporate` THEN 1000
    OTHERWISE 500

GIVEN `the offender` IS A Offender
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment in months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offender`
    WHEN `a body corporate` THEN 0
    OTHERWISE 6

GIVEN `the Public Prosecutor consented in writing beforehand` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a prosecution may be instituted` IF
    `the Public Prosecutor consented in writing beforehand`

§§ `6 and 7 -- police powers`

-- s 6: "Any police officer may arrest without warrant any person whom he has reasonable cause to believe has committed or is
-- committing an offence."
-- s 7: a police officer "may enter any premises where any foreign national emblem is displayed contrary to the provisions of this Act"
-- or of any s 5 order or permit "or any school in which he has reasonable cause to believe that an offence is being or has been
-- committed", and may search and seize evidence. READING (an inference from the sentence's structure): the "reasonable cause" test is
-- attached only to schools; for other premises the text requires the emblem actually to be displayed contrary to the Act.
GIVEN `the officer has reasonable cause to believe an offence was or is being committed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer may arrest without warrant` IF
    `the officer has reasonable cause to believe an offence was or is being committed`

GIVEN `an emblem is displayed there contrary to the Act or a permit` IS A BOOLEAN, `the place is a school` IS A BOOLEAN, `reasonable cause to believe an offence there` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer may enter and search` IF
        `an emblem is displayed there contrary to the Act or a permit`
    OR  (`the place is a school` AND `reasonable cause to believe an offence there`)
